## 1. Introduction
Rechargeable Zn-air batteries (ZAB) characterized by high theoretical energy density, high energy conversion efficiency and environmentally friendly have been considered as one of most promising next-generation electrical energy storage and conversion devices [1-4]. However, the low kinetics of oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER) require high energy to overcome this barrier so as to achieve high work efficiency. To date, the $\sf P t/C$ and $\mathsf{R u O}_{2}/\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ have been recognized as activity criterions for single ORR and OER, respectively [5-7]. Nevertheless, the defects of noble-metal catalysts, including limited storage content, low stability and single reactivity [8,9], compel us to exploit novel bi-functional electrochemical catalysts.
Transition metals, such as Fe, Co, Ni, have similar unsaturated $d$ - atomic orbitals to noble metal elements, which is beneficial to gain and loss of electrons, thus exhibiting great potentials to substitute the noble-metal catalysts. Currently, tremendous efforts have been devoted to develop and design transition metal catalysts for ORR and OER, such as Fe, Co, Ni and their alloys, metal oxide, nitride, sulfide, and a great success has been achieved including relative high performance and high stability [10-13]. In spite of this, these catalysts have also continued to suffer from the metal nanoparticle aggregation and finite durability, which makes it hard to maximize the catalytic activity of catalysts [14-16]. Moreover recently, considerable attentions have been focused on the rare-earth metal oxides like $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ , which is characterized by plentiful oxygen vacancies and can be efficient to strengthen the metal-carrier interactions [17-19]. Therefore, it is a smart strategy to achieve high stable oxygen electrocatalysts by the efficient integration of rare-earth metal oxides into transition-metal active sites.
Beyond the metal active sites, the support materials also correlate closely with the activity of catalysts. Undoubtedly, the most usual support in electrochemical field is heteroatoms (i.e. N, S, P, B) doped carbon matrix since the doping of heteroatoms can efficiently modulate the electron-spin patterns and improve the electron donating ability of carbon atoms [20-24]. Nevertheless, the existed template-calcining methods always create a mass of defective carbon layers, which is in favor of the formation of more active sites according to the recent researches [25], but it also triggers low intrinsic conductivity and unsatisfactory catalytic activity simultaneously. Comparing with the defective carbon layers, graphitic carbon exhibits stronger electron conductivity owing to the existence of inherent infinite $\pi$ conjugated systems [1]. But it’s hard to obtain high graphitization carbon layers through simply calcining individual template at high temperature in a short time. Therefore, it is a valid strategy to involve the primitive graphitic carbon materials into the calcining templates to establish high graphitization degree catalysts. At present, the combination of rare earth metal oxides $(\mathsf{C e O}_{2})$ and transition metals with graphene oxide (GO) layers is rarely reported in the field of electrochemical ORR/OER. Thus, the exploration of this type of candidate catalysts is still urgently needed.
Herein, we established a multistage mixed growth template method to construct the Ce-doped Fe-based hierarchical catalysts $({\mathrm{Fe-Ce@GSL}})$ by first fabricating the Fe-Ni-LDH linked MOFs and GO sheets as template (MOF/LDH/GO) and then calcining it at high temperature. Different from normal MOF/GO composites, the LDHs (hydrotalcites) have been designed on the surface of GO to link the MOFs and GO sheets in this work, successfully avoiding the separation of MOFs derivatives and GO during pyrolysis process. Moreover, the hierarchical structure benefits to expose more active sites and improve mass transfer [26]. Specifically, the LDHderivatives sandwiched between MOFs-derivatives and GO sheets promotes the formation of multiphase interfaces, which may be advantageous to boost the electrocatalytic performances through subtly modulating the electronic features [27]. Beyond this, rare earth metal oxides $(\mathsf{C e O}_{2})$ are characterized with different electronic structures like $4\mathrm{f}^{1}5\mathrm{d}^{0}\ (\mathrm{Ce}^{3+})$ and $4\mathrm{f}^{0}5\mathrm{d}^{0}\ (\mathrm{Ce}^{4+})$ , in which the electron can easy switch in $d$ and $f$ orbitals thus creating abundant oxygen vacancies[28]. As we mentioned above, the oxygen vacancies are strongly correlative to the electrochemical performance and the stability. Benefit from these merits, the optimal $\mathrm{Fe}_{7.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ catalysts exhibit enhanced ORR/OER performances and longer durability than that of the pristine $\operatorname{Fe@GSL-}$ 800 catalysts. Moreover, the aqueous and solid-state rechargeable Zn-air batteries assembled with $\mathrm{Fe}_{7.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ cathode catalysts deliver outstanding performances with high peak power densities, high specific capacities and ultra-long charge–discharge durability (aqueous $\mathrm{ZAB}~>~500~\mathrm{\h}/3000$ cycles $@$ $10~\mathrm{\mA.cm^{-2}}$ ; solid-state $\mathrm{ZAB}>230\ \mathrm{h}/1380$ cycles $\textcircled{\underline{{\theta}}}2\mathrm{\mA.cm}^{-2}$ ).
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. The synthesis of LDH/GO composite
Typically, 0.37 mmol ( $150\mathrm{mg})$ of $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and $0.90\mathrm{mmol}$ $(262\mathrm{mg})$ of ${\mathsf{N i}}({\mathsf{N O}}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6{\mathsf{H}}_{2}0$ were dissolved into $36~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol and deionized water mixture (VCH3OH: ${\mathsf{V}}_{\mathrm{H20}}=5{:}1$ ) to obtain solution A. 1.37 mmol $(500~\mathrm{mg},\$ of cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB) and $0.61\mathrm{\mmol}$ $\cdot50\ \mathrm{mg})$ of 2-methylimidazole (2-MeIm) were dispersed in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to form solution B. Then the solutions A and B were transferred into a hydrothermal reactor and stirred for $20\ \mathrm{\min}$ followed by the hydrothermal process at $140^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ . The obtained products denote as Fe-Ni-LDH.
After cooling down to room temperature, $10~\mathrm{mg}$ of graphene oxide sheets (GO) which is synthesized as we previous reported procedures [29] was added into above Fe-Ni-LDH solution and treated by ultrasonic processing, then the reaction was performed at $90\ \mathrm{^\circC}$ for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The separated solid products were washed with methanol three times and dried at $60~^{\circ}C$ in a vacuum overnight. The products were denoted as Fe-Ni-LDH/GO.
## 2.2. The synthesis of Fe-Ce-MOF/LDH/GO precursors
Typically, the dried Fe-Ni-LDH/GO was dispersed into $15~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water and treated by ultrasonic treatment for $30~\mathrm{min}$ (denoted as solution A). Then $15~\mathrm{mL}$ of the $\mathrm{FeSO_{4}}$ ( $3.5\mathrm{\mmol}$ ) and $\mathrm{Eu}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ $\left\langle0.5\mathrm{\mmol}\right\rangle$ mixtures were added into solution A to form the solution B. Subsequently, the 1,10-phenanthroline monohydrate $(\mathsf{C}_{12}\mathsf{H}_{8}\mathsf{N}_{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ 3 mmol) and 3,5- pyridinedicarboxylic acid $(\mathsf{C}_{7}\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{N O}_{4}$ , 3 mmol) organic ligands were dissolved into $70~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water and treated with ultrasonic treatment for $60\ \mathrm{min}$ (denoted as solution C). Finally, the solution B and C were mixed together followed by adding $1.5~\mathrm{mL}$ of NaOH (2.5 M) solution into the mixture. Then the mixture was stirred vigorously for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ and then placed into an oven at $90~^{\circ}C$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ and the obtained solid products were denoted as Fe-Ce-MOF/LDH/GO materials.
## 2.3. The synthesis of Fe-Ce@GSL-T (where t represents the calcining temperature)
## 3. Results and discussion
The construction of Fe-Ce@GSL catalysts is realized via multistage mixed growth template method, which is illustrated in Fig. 1a. At first, the precursors of Fe-Ce-MOF were self-assembled on the surface of LDH/GO carriers to establish the MOF/LDH/GO sandwich templates, and then the template calcining approach was employed to achieve the ${\mathrm{Fe-Ce@GSL}}$ catalysts. The structure and morphology of LDH/GO templates and Fe-Ce@GSL materials were revealed by Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM). Fig. 1b reveals that the Fe-Ni-LDH nanoplatelets with the diameter of $\approx$ $200\ \mathrm{nm}$ homogenously distributed on the surface of graphene oxide sheet renders the surface become rough and modifiable. While the $\mathrm{Fe}_{7}{-}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}{-}800$ catalysts exhibit a typical 2D porous structure, where many network MOFs-derivatives are anchored to both sides of the graphene oxide sheets (Fig. 2c-e). Meanwhile, the graphene oxide sheets can be easily identified from the interlamination of catalysts, indicating the successful integration of MOFs with GO sheets. Notably, the porous architecture of the mesh MOFs-derivatives should be ascribed to the shrink of MOFs during pyrolysis process at high temperature. In order to confirm the composition of $\mathrm{Fe}_{7}{-}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}{-}800$ , the elemental mapping and X-ray energy dispersive spectrum (EDS) were performed. Apparently, all of elements, including O, N, Fe, Ce and Ni, are uniformly dispersed in the catalysts (Fig. 1g-l), further proving the formation of sandwich configuration. Notably, the content of Ni in elemental mapping and EDS pattern is much lower than other elements, indicating that most of LDHs are covered by the surficial Fe-Ce-MOF derivatives (Fig. 1l).
The details of internal construction of $\mathrm{Fe}_{7}–\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ catalysts were revealed by the Transmission Electron Microscopy (TEM). Fig. 2a-c disclose that copious tiny metal nanoparticles with the mean-size of $\sim8.21\ \mathrm{nm}$ are uniformly interspersed in the catalysts, in which some fragmentary graphene nanoplatelets can be identified. The magnified TEM results reveal that the lattice spacing of $0.33~\mathrm{{nm}}$ correlates to the (002) plane of graphene, which is surrounded by many metal nanoclusters (Fig. 2d-e) [30]. Moreover, further enlarged TEM images disclose that these metal nanoclusters possess various lattice planes with diverse interplanar spacing (Fig. 2f). The lattice spacing of $0.209{\mathrm{~nm}}$ and $0.192\ \mathrm{nm}$ in the outer space should be assigned to (400) plane of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}$ and (220) of ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ [31,32], which should be derived from the carbonization of surficial Fe-Ce-MOF. In the inner region, the clear lattice fringes with the interplanar distance of $0.206~\mathrm{\nm}$ and $0.149~\mathrm{nm}$ are relevant to the (111) and (200) planes of FeNi alloy [33,34], which should be originated from the interior Fe-Ni-LDH phase. Moreover, there is a distinct interface appearing at the junction between MOFs derivatives and LDHs derivatives, which may be caused by the edge shrink of different parental frameworks at high temperature, since the shrinking ration of different organic ligands is diverse at the same conditions [35]. The interface is supposed to fine-tune the electronic recombination efficiency so as to enrich the interface electron conductivity and electrocatalytic performances [27]. Moreover, this phenomenon firmly confirms that MOFs are successfully bonded to the surface of LDH/GO, and the Fe-Ni-LDH plays a pivotal role in connecting the GO and MOFs tightly.
For further confirming the constitution of each catalyst, the XRay Powder Diffraction (XRD) was carried out. As shown in Fig. 3a, Fe-MOF/LDH/GO derived $\mathsf{F e@G S L-800}$ catalyst exhibits two types of peaks. The peaks located at $43.680^{\circ}$ , $50.814^{\circ}$ and $74.725^{\circ}$ should be assigned to the [111], [200] and [220] planes of $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.64}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.36}$ alloy phase [PDF#47–1405], while the peaks appeared at $44.836^{\circ}$ and $65.181^{\circ}$ match well with the [400] and [440] planes of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ species [PDF#26–1136], indicating the coexistence of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}$ and FeNi alloy species. By the way, there is a weak peak located at $35.191^{\circ}$ , which is relevant to the [0 4 0] plane of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}[\mathrm{PDF}\#16\mathrm{-}0653]$ , suggesting the existence of various valence state of Fe species, which is beneficial to gain and loss electrons and thus improving the ORR/OER performances.
For the Ce-MOF/LDH/GO derived $\mathsf{C e@G S L-800}$ catalyst, the distinct peaks situated at $28.359^{\circ}$ , $32.930^{\circ}$ , $47.398^{\circ}$ , $56.239^{\circ}$ , $69.400^{\circ}$ and $76.634^{\circ}$ correspond to the [1 1 1], [2 0 0], [2 2 0], [3 1 1], [4 0 0] and [331] planes of ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ [PDF#34–0394]. Moreover, the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}$ and FeNi alloy species also can be detected with a low peak intensity, which may be related to the coverage factor of surficial CeMOF-derivatives. Interestingly, Fe-Ce or Ni-Ce alloy are not perceived in the XRD pattern due to the extremely strong oxygen affinity of rare-earth metal atoms [36,37]. The XRD pattern of $\mathrm{Fe}_{7}{-}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}{-}800$ discloses that the characteristic peaks of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ , FeNi alloy and $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ phases coexist in the catalysts, but the content of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ is relative low comparing with other comparative substances.
The Raman spectra have been conducted to understand the graphitization degree of carbon layers. As shown in Fig. 3b, the peaks of D-band and G-band represent lattice defective carbon and $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ -hybridized graphitic carbon [38], whereas the intensity ratio $\left(\mathrm{I_{D}}/\mathrm{I_{G}}\right)$ of D-band $\left(\mathrm{I_{D}}\right)$ and G-band $\left(\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{G}}\right)$ reflect the graphitization degree of carbon layers. The $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ values of Ce@GSL-800, Fe@GSL800 and $\mathrm{Fe}_{7}–\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ catalysts are 0.85, 0.94 and 0.96, implying the high graphitization degree, which is in favor of the electron transfer. Meanwhile, the $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ value of $\mathrm{Fe}_{7}{-}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}{-}800$ slightly higher than the $\mathrm{Fe@GSL-800}$ and $\mathsf{C e@G S L-800}$ catalysts, indicating the more defective carbon, which may be caused by the mismatching of metal-atom radius $[\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{Fe}}=1.26\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ , $\mathsf{D}_{\mathsf{C e}}=1.88\ \mathring{\mathsf{A}}]$ , thus leading to the formation of structural defects [39].
BET specific surface area and pore structure are inspected by the $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms. The typical type-IV isotherm curves are observed for $\mathrm{Fe@GSL-800}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{7}–\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ materials (Fig. 3c), and the BET surface areas are calculated to be $154.7~\mathrm{m}^{2}.\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $195.5~\mathrm{m}^{2}.\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. Moreover, the porous configuration features with the main pore sizes of $5{-}10~\mathrm{nm}$ for Fe@GSL-800 and $\mathrm{Fe}_{7}{-}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}{-}800$ can be proved by the pore size distribution plots, such mesporous structure may facilitate to electrolyte/O-containing species transfer.
In order to further understand the constitution of $\mathrm{Fe}_{7}–\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL-}$ 800 catalysts, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurement was performed. The high resolution C 1s spectrum can be deconvoluted into three sub-peaks corresponding to $c{\mathrm{-}}c$ (284.5 eV), CAN (285.0 eV) and CAO (286.0 eV) bonds (Fig. 3d), respectively [40,41]. The N 1s spectrum can be divided into four types of species, the peaks centered at 398.26 eV, 400.68 eV, $402.18\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $405.25\ \mathrm{eV}$ with the area ratio of 1: 1.4: 0.9: 0.5 are referred to as pyridinic-N, pyrrolic-N, graphitic-N and oxidized-N (Fig. 3e), respectively [7,42]. Remarkably, the total contents of graphitic-N and pyridinic-N are similar to the pyrrolic-N and oxidized-N, which may be in favor of improving the electrochemical performances. The high resolution O 1s spectrum exhibits three peaks centered at 530.1 eV, 531.1 eV and 532.3 eV (Fig. 3f), which correlates to the metal-O, adsorbed-O and $0-C=0$ bonds, respectively [43-45]. The metal-O species are derived from the metal oxides according to the XRD results, while the $0-C=0$ comes from the organic ligands of MOFs. The adsorbed O may be derived from the coordination of free oxygen with defect sites of catalysts, indicating a large amount of oxygen vacancies exist on the surface of catalysts. To gain deeper insights into the oxygen vacancies, the electron paramagnetic response spectroscopy (EPR) test was performed. The obvious signal at the position of $\mathbf{g}=2.036$ for $\mathrm{Fe}_{7}.$ $\mathsf{C e}_{1}@\mathsf{G S L}\cdot800$ catalysts valid elucidates the existence of oxygen vacancies in this sample (inset of Fig. 3f) [46].
Fe $\mathsf{2p}$ XPS spectrum can be refitted into two sets of peaks of $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ (Fig. 3g), the sub-peaks located at $710.5~\mathrm{~eV}$ · $712.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $718.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ match well with the $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ of $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ and the satellite peaks, while the peaks located at $723.9\ \mathrm{~eV}$ ， $725.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $732.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ belong to the $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ of $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ and the satellite peaks, respectively, demonstrating the coexistence of $^+2$ and $^+3$ valent state Fe species in catalysts [47-50]. Combining the XPS results with the XRD and TEM results we can firmly believe the formation of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ phases. The ${\mathsf{C e}}^{4+}$ presents six peaks composed of 3 spin–orbit doublets owing to various Ce 4f occupancies post-excitation [32]. The peaks of $881.9\ \mathrm{eV},890.0\ \mathrm{eV},897.7\ \mathrm{eV},$ 900.8 eV, 907.6 eV and $917.0\mathrm{eV}$ are related to ${\mathsf{C e}}^{4+}$ , while the peaks of $884.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ and 904.1 eV should be assigned to the $\mathsf{C e}^{3+}$ [51-53]. The existence of dual oxidation state demonstrates that the ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ features with rich surface lattice defects and vacancies [53], which is advantageous to enrich the electrochemical performances. This conclusion can be supported by the O 1s XPS results and EPR spectrum. The survey spectrum shown in Fig. 3i reveals that all elements including C, N, O, Fe and Ce can be identified from the survey spectrum except for Ni (LDH derivatives), indicating that the containing-Ni species mainly exist in the middle position of catalysts, and this results can be demonstrated by the highresolution spectrum of Ni 2p (Fig. S1).
The ORR performances of catalysts were evaluated by linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) in alkaline medium (0.1 M KOH). The influences of molar ratio of $\mathsf{F e/C e}$ and the calcining temperatures were firstly considered. As shown in Fig. S2, the Fe-Ce@GSL-800 catalysts synthesized at $800^{\circ}\mathsf C$ with Fe/Ce molar ratio of 7:1 exhibit the highest ORR performance, affording the most positive onset potential $\left(\mathrm{E_{onset}}\right)$ and half-wave potential $\left(\mathbb{E}_{1/2}\right)$ $\mathrm{'E_{onset}=0.977\ V}$ , $\mathrm{E}_{1/2}=0.870\:\mathrm{V})$ , and the half-wave potential is $20~\mathrm{mV}$ better than the monometallic $\mathrm{Fe@GSL-800}$ $\mathrm{'}\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{onset}}=0.940\mathrm{~V~}$ , $\mathrm{E}_{1/2}=0.850\mathrm{~V)}$ and commercial $\sf P t/C$ $\left(\mathrm{E_{onset}}\:=\:0.993\:\mathrm{\V}\right.$ , $\mathrm{E}_{1/2}=0.850\mathrm{~V)}$ catalysts (Fig. 4a, b). The Tafel slope was calculated to examine the inherent reaction dynamics of catalysts toward ORR. Apparently, the Tafel slope of $\mathrm{Fe}_{7.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ catalysts is merely $50\ \mathrm{mV.dec^{-1}}$ , lower than the $\sf P t/C$ $(76~\mathrm{mV.dec^{-1}}.$ and $\mathrm{Fe@GSL-800}$ $(58\ \mathrm{mV.dec^{-1}}$ ) catalysts (Fig. 4c), implying the fastest kinetics occurred on the $\mathrm{Fe}_{7}$ -- $\mathsf{C e}_{1}@\mathsf{G S L}800$ catalysts towards ORR.
To have more insights into the ORR kinetics of $\mathrm{Fe}_{7.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ catalysts, the LSV curves recorded at different rotation speeds were analyzed. As usual, the current density increases with the improvement of rotation speeds since the acceleration of transfer speeding of O-containing species (Fig. 4d). The corresponding KouteckyLevich (K-L) plots parallel with each other prove the excellent linear correlation (inset of Fig. 4d), and the average electron transfer number (n) per oxygen molecule is calculated 3.84, indicating that the electron transfer follows the ideal 4-electron pathway.
In order to gain deep insights into the catalytic active sites of ORR, the comparative experiments were carried out. As displayed in Fig. S3, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{7.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ is observed to have a better halfwave potential than that of $\mathrm{Fe@GSL-800}$ catalysts, implying that the incorporation of Ce oxides have a positive influence on the ORR performance. Furthermore, it is well-known that Fe species is very sensitive to $\mathsf{S C N^{-}}$ ions owing to the strong coordination ability of $\mathtt{S C N}^{-}[29,54]$ . Therefore, the $\mathsf{S C N^{-}}$ was introduced into the electrolyte to notarize the role of Fe species. Apparently, both $\mathrm{Fe@GSL-800}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{7.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ catalysts deliver gravely decreased ORR activities in the presence of $\mathsf{S C N^{-}}$ , proving the ORR active sites are blocked by the formed $\mathsf{S C N^{-}}$ complex, and also confirming that the Fe-containing nanoparticles are the mainly active sites. Moreover, the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LDH}}/{\mathrm{GO}}$ carrier derivatives (denoted as LG-D) where the molar ratio of Ni/Fe is 2.4:1 exhibit very poor ORR activity, meanwhile, the ORR performance is observed to no obvious decay in the presence of SCN- (Fig. S3), demonstrating the introduction of Ni-species has little effect on promoting ORR activity.
The stability of catalysts was subsequently tested with chronoamperometry (i-t). As displayed in Fig. 4e, after $30000\ {\mathfrak{s}}$ continuous testing, the chronoamperometric response of the Fe@GSL-800 and $\mathrm{Fe}_{7.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ catalysts exhibit the smallest changes with only $7.2\%$ degradations, demonstrating the superb stability of $\mathrm{Fe@GSL-800}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{7.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ catalysts.
As another well-known indispensable reaction of rechargeable $Z\mathrm{n}$ -air battery, the OER performance was also evaluated by LSV in $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH electrolyte. As shown in Fig. S4, the catalysts with high Fe molar ratios exhibit little differences in OER performances at the current density of $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . $(\mathrm{Fe}_{7.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ , $\mathfrak{n}=360$ $\mathrm{mv}$ ; Fe@GSL-800, $\upeta=366~\mathrm{mV}$ ; $\mathrm{Fe}_{3.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ , $\mathrm{\Delta\eta=380~mV}$ ; $\mathrm{Fe}_{1.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ , $\mathrm{\Delta\eta}\mathrm{\Delta}\eta=378\mathrm{\mV}$ ; $\mathrm{Fe}_{1.}\mathrm{Ce}_{3}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ , $\upeta=452\mathrm{~mV}$ ; $\mathsf{C e@G S L-800}$ , $\upeta=544\mathrm{mV}.$ ). Impressively, the performance of optimal $\mathrm{Fe}_{7.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ $\mathrm{\Delta\cdot\n=360~mV}$ ) catalyst is very close to the $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ $\upeta=353~\mathrm{mV},$ ) (Fig. 4f). Remarkably, the potential difference $(\Delta\mathrm{E}=\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{j}=10}-\mathrm{E}_{1/2})$ between ORR $\left(\mathsf{E}_{1/2}\right)$ and OER $\left(\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{j}=10}\right)$ of $\mathrm{Fe}_{7}\mathrm{.Ce}_{1}@.$ GSL-800 catalysts is only $0.720\mathrm{V}$ , even better than the $\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf{C}+\mathsf{R u O}_{2}$ mixture (0.734 V) and most of other catalysts (Table S1). Additionally, the OER durability was also investigated with chronopotentiometry (CP) at the current density of $10\ \mathrm{mA.cm^{-2}}$ . Apparently, after $20\mathrm{~h~}$ operation, the change of current density of $\mathrm{Fe}_{7.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@-$ GSL-800 catalysts is merely $0.41\%$ , much lower than the $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ $(7.21\%)$ catalysts and the pure Fe@GSL-800 $(3.07\%)$ catalysts (Fig. 4h), indicating that the doping of Ce can efficiently enhance the stability of catalysts.
In order to further understand the origination of the high activity of catalysts, the ECSA (electrochemically active surface area) has been evaluated by $\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{dl}}$ (electrical double layer capacitance) which positive correlates to with the ECSA. As presented in Fig. 4i and Fig. S5, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{7.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ catalysts possess relatively high $\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{dl}}$ $_{\mathrm{~l~}}(\mathbf{C_{dl}}=12.71~\mathrm{mF.cm^{-2}}.$ , further demonstrating the potential of high electrochemical performances. Moreover, the catalytic active sites of OER were also inspected. $\mathsf{F e@G S L-800}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{7.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@.$ - GSL-800 catalysts display a very similar OER performance (Fig. S6), indicating that the introduction of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ has little impact on the OER performance. However, Fe@GSL-800 and $\mathrm{Fe}_{7.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ catalysts suffer from a great loss of OER performance in the presence of $S C\mathsf{N}^{-}$ , confirming that the most valid active sites are Fespecies instead of Ce. Particularly, the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LDH}}/{\mathrm{GO}}$ carrier derivatives (denoted as LG-D) also exhibit flexible response to the $\mathsf{S C N^{-}}$ , indicating the synergy of Ni and Fe species can be identified as one of positive factors to promote the OER property of catalyst. In summary, the excellent performances of $\mathrm{Fe}_{7.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ should be attributed to the large electrochemical active areas, advantageous configuration and fully exposed active sites, and the synergetic effect of different species.
Considering of the excellent ORR and OER performances of $\mathsf{F e}_{7-}.$ - $\mathsf{C e}_{1}@\mathsf{G S L}800$ catalysts, the home-made aqueous Zn-air battery (ZAB) has assembled (Fig. 5a and Fig. S7), as contrast, the $\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf{\Lambda}$ $\textsf{C}+\textsf{R u O}_{2}$ based $Z\mathrm{n}$ -air battery has also constructed at the same condition. The open circuit voltage (OCV) of ZAB catalyzed by $\mathrm{Fe}_{7-}-$ $\mathsf{C e}_{1}@\mathsf{G S L}\cdot800$ catalysts is $1.56\mathrm{~V~}$ , slightly higher than the $\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf{\Lambda}$ $\textsf{C}+\textsf{R u O}_{2}$ based ZAB (Fig. 5b). Two $\mathrm{Fe}_{7.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ based ZABs can afford a $2\mathrm{~V~}$ LED lamp (inset of Fig. 2b). Moreover, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{7-}-$ $\mathsf{C e}_{1}@\mathsf{G S L}–800$ based ZAB also delivers higher peak power density (194 $\ E\quad\mathrm{m}\mathrm{w}.\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ) and smaller voltage gap according to the charge–discharge polarization curve (Fig. 5c-d). The discharge curves prove that the ZAB with $\mathrm{Fe}_{7.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ as catalysts exhibits better stability with the voltage almost unchanged after $15\mathrm{~h~}$ continuous discharge at the current density of $10~\mathrm{\mA.cm^{-2}}$ (Fig. 5e). When normalized the mass of consumed Zn plate, the specific capacity of ZABs can be obtained. As displayed in Fig. 5f, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{7.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ based ZAB exhibits ultra-high specific capacity $(801.1\mathrm{\mA}.\mathrm{h}/\mathrm{g})$ , much higher than the $\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-RuO}_{2}$ based ZAB $(778.5\ \mathrm{mA}.\mathrm{h}/\mathrm{g})$ . Meanwhile, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{7.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ based ZAB also shows a high-sensitive rate-performance with small voltage-drop with the increase of current density (Fig. 5g). Finally, the longterm charge–discharge performances have been evaluated. Apparently, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{7.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ based ZAB exhibits as long as over $500\mathrm{~h~}$ long-term charge–discharge stability at the current density of $10\ \mathrm{mA.cm^{-2}}$ (Fig. 5h), and the energy efficiency $\mathrm{Fe}_{7}–\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL-}$ 800 based ZAB still maintains of $55.2\%$ , much superior to the ZAB catalyzed by $\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-RuO}_{2}$ physical mixture (energy efficiency changes from $56.4\%$ to $51.7\%$ ). Most importantly, the properties including OCV, maximum power density, specific capacity and stability of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{7.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ based ZAB better than most of reported systems (Table S2). Specifically, the surface morphology of used catalysts is inspected by SEM (Fig. S8). Apparently, the catalysts still present typical layered configuration without corrosion, which should be attributed to the encapsulation effect of outer carbon layers. Moreover, the pure Fe-based materials assembled ZAB also evaluated under the similar conditions. As displayed in Fig. S9, the $\operatorname{Fe@}$ GSL-800 based ZAB exhibits as good as $\mathrm{Fe}_{7.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ based ZAB high performances, except an inferior peak power density $179.4\mathrm{\mW.cm^{-2}}$ ).
Inspired by the high performances of aqueous ZAB, the more practical all solid-state ZAB has been constructed (Fig. 6a). The solid-state $\mathsf{F e}_{7.\mathsf{C e}_{1}}\ @\mathsf{G S L}–800$ ZAB exhibits excellent high OCV (1.43 V) and peak power density $(94.2\mathrm{\mW.cm^{-2}}.$ , much superior to the $\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-RuO}_{2}$ based ZAB (1.40 V; $74\mathrm{\mw}.\mathrm{cm}^{-2}.$ ) (Fig. 6b, c). The rate-performances of $\mathrm{Fe}_{7.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ based ZAB and $\mathrm{Pt/C_{-}}$ $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ based ZAB are shown in Fig. 6d. Clearly, the two ZABs exhibit flexible dynamic response, and the discharge voltage platform are always close to $1.2\mathrm{V}$ even at $10\mathrm{mA.cm}^{-2}$ . The long-time discharge properties are evaluated at the current density of $5\mathrm{\mA.cm^{-2}}$ , after $^{10\mathrm{h}}$ continuous discharge, the changes of voltage platform almost can’t be detected (Fig. 6e). Meanwhile, the specific capacities of $\mathrm{Fe}_{7.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ based ZAB and $\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-RuO}_{2}$ based ZAB are obtained by normalized the consumed mass of Zn plates. As shown in Fig. 6f, both $\mathrm{Fe}_{7.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ based ZAB and $\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-RuO}_{2}$ based ZAB exhibit as high as $791.7\mathrm{\mA.h/g}$ and $755.1\ \mathrm{\mA.h/g}$ specific capacities, respectively. The charge–discharge polarization curves imply the trend of voltage gap of charge–discharge process, obviously, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{7.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ based ZAB may possess a smaller voltage gap according to the charge–discharge polarization curves in Fig. 6g.
Finally, the charge–discharge performances have been investigated as shown in Fig. 6h. The ZAB assembled with $\mathrm{Fe}_{7.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL-}$ 800 as cathode catalysts exhibits narrower potential gap and a long-term cycling capability $(>260\ \mathrm{h})$ at the current density of $2\mathrm{mA}.\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ with $10\mathrm{min}$ per cycle, and the energy efficiency of $\mathrm{Fe}_{7}.$ -- $\mathsf{C e}_{1}@\mathsf{G S L}\cdot800$ based ZAB merely changes $3.6\%$ (from $64.2\%$ to $60.6\%$ after ${\tt>}200{\tt\ h}$ operation. While $\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-RuO}_{2}$ catalyzed ZAB has been demonstrated to have an inferior charge–discharge capability at the same test conditions, further confirming the excellent activity and stability of $\mathrm{Fe}_{7.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ catalysts. The performances of $\mathrm{Fe}_{7.}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ ZAB is also superior to the $\mathrm{Fe@GSL-800}$ based ZAB (Fig. S10) and many other reported catalysts catalyzed ZABs (Table S3.) Most importantly, two solid-state ZABs can afford a $2\mathrm{~V~}$ LED lamp for ${\cdot}{>}60\mathrm{~h~}$ (Fig. S11), indicating the strong practical application ability.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, we have established the multistage mixed growth method to construct the LDH linked GO sheets and MOFs $(\mathsf{M O F}/$ LDH/GO) composite, with this composite as template, the highlyefficient ${\mathrm{Fe-Ce@GSL}}$ catalysts have been obtained. SEM results prove that the LDH are evenly distributed on the surface of GO sheet, and the ${\mathrm{Fe-Ce@GSL}}$ catalysts possess hierarchical structure. Moreover, experimental results disclose that LDH derivatives play the role of linking MOFs derivatives and GO sheets, which render the formation of interface between different species. The optimal $\mathrm{Fe}_{7}–\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}–800$ catalysts exhibit superb ORR $(\mathrm{E}_{1/2}\:=\:0.870\:\:\mathrm{V})$ and OER $\left(\mathrm{E}_{j=10}=1.590\:\mathrm{V}\right)$ performances with the potential difference between ORR and OER of $0.720\mathrm{V},$ , superior to the $\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-RuO}_{2}$ catalysts. Inspired by the excellent ORR and OER activities, the aqueous and solid-state ZABs were assembled. Both aqueous and solid-state $\mathrm{Fe}_{7}{-}\mathrm{Ce}_{1}@\mathrm{GSL}{-}800$ based ZABs exhibit high OCV, high peak power density and specific capacities as well as ultra-stable charge–discharge stability. Combining the advantages of unique hierarchical structure with the magnificent electrochemical performances, we can deduce that this strategy and this type of catalysts may play a remarkable role in the process of the commercialization of ZABs.
